The Workers in the Vineyard
The parable of the Kingdom of Heaven is like a man who had a plot of land. He went out early in the morning to hire laborers for his vineyard.
He agreed to pay them the standard daily wage for a day's work, and then sent them to his vineyards.
He went out again around 9 a.m. He saw others standing in the market doing nothing.
He told them, "You too, go and work in my vineyards. I will give you the wage you deserve."
And so they left. "He went out again around noon and at three o'clock and did the same thing.
Around five o'clock, he went out and found others standing there. He asked them, "Why have you been standing here all day doing nothing?"
They answered, "Because no one hired us." He said to them, "You also go and work in my vineyard."
And when evening came, the owner of the vineyard spoke to the one who was in charge of the workers and said to him, "Go out to the workers and give them their pay, starting with the ones who were hired last, and then going to the ones who were hired first."
The workers who were employed at 5:00 PM arrived. Each one of them took their normal daily wage.
And just as the first workers came, they were expecting to get more. But each one of them got the same normal daily wage.
And when they took possession, they began to complain about the owner.
They said: "These are the people who were hired last, they only worked for an hour, and I gave them the same pay as us. We worked more and stayed in the scorching sun all day."
The owner answered one of them. He said, "My friend, I am dealing with you fairly. Did you not agree to work for the usual daily wage?"
Take your money and leave. I want to give the people I last hired the same pay I gave you.
