There will be great torment in those days. It will be worse than any other torment from the beginning of the world until now. And there will never be anything like it again.
If time wasn't cut short, nobody would survive. But it will be cut short because of God's chosen people.
At that time, someone might say to you, 'Look, here is the Messiah!' or 'There he is!' But don't believe it.
The Antichrist and the false prophets will appear. They will perform miracles and great signs. They will try to deceive the elect people of God if possible.
Look, I told you this before.
So, if someone tells you, 'He is in the distant desert,' do not go there. Or if someone says, 'He is in the depths of the house,' do not believe it.
The lightning that comes from the east will be visible in the west. That is how it will be when the Son of Man comes.
Vultures gather wherever there is a dead body.
After the terrible ordeal that happened in those days, "the sun will be dark, and the moon will not give its light; and the stars will fall from the heaven, and the powers of the heavens will be shaken." (Isaiah 13:10; 34:4)
"Then the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven, and all the nations of the earth will mourn. They will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven, with great power and glory.
To send His angels with a loud trumpet call. To gather His chosen ones from the four corners of the earth. To bring them from one end of the heavens to the other.
Learn from the fig tree; just as its branches wither and its leaves sprout, so you may know that summer is near.
In the same way, when you see all these things happening, you know that the end is near. It is at hand.
What I am going to tell you is true. The people who were living at that time will not die before all these things happen.
The heaven and the earth shall pass away: but my words shall not pass away. We know not the day nor the hour.
No one knows the day or the hour. Even the angels in heaven do not know. The Son does not know. Only the Father knows.
Remember how the days were in the time of Noah. So it will be when the Son of Man comes.
In the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking. They were marrying. They were giving their daughters in marriage. They did all these things until the day that Noah entered the ark.
They did not know anything about what was going to happen until the floods came and took them all. This is how it will be when the Son of Man comes.
Two men will be in the field. One will be taken, and the other will be left.
Two women are grinding with a hand mill, and one of them is taken and the other is left.
Therefore, be watchful, because you do not know the day your Lord will come.
You need to understand something. If the homeowner knew when the thief would come at night, he would be watching. He would not let his house be burglarized.
So, you also need to be ready, for the Son of Man is going to come at an hour you do not expect.
Let us imagine that a master has appointed one of his servants to supervise the other servants in his house. The servant's task is to give them their food at the appropriate time. The master wants a servant who is faithful and intelligent to carry out this task.
It would be better for the servant if, upon the master's return, he found him diligently attending to his duties.
What I am going to tell you is true. The master is going to give this servant responsibility over everything he owns.
But suppose that this servant is wicked. And suppose that he said to himself: 'My master is going to be away for a long time.'
Suppose he starts hitting the other servants. And suppose he starts eating and drinking with those who drink excessively.
This man's slave will return on a day that the man does not expect. He will return at a time that the man does not know.
And after that, they will cut Mr. Hall into pieces. And to send him to the place where the liars go. And everyone will cry and grind their teeth.
The Tale of Ten Maids of Honour
That is the parable of the Kingdom of Heaven at that time. Ten bridesmaids took their lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them were stupid. Five were wise.
The poor people took their lanterns, but they didn't take any olive oil with them.
The poor people took the oil in bottles with their lanterns.
The groom didn't come for a long time. That's how everyone got tired, the bridesmaids and they dozed off.
In the middle of the night, someone shouted: "Here is the groom! Come out to meet him!"
After that, all the bridesmaids surpassed her and prepared their lamps.
The foolish said to the wise, "Give us some of your oil, for our lamps are going out."
"No," they answered. "There may not be enough for all of us. Go instead to those who are selling oil. Buy some for yourselves."
And they went to buy oil. But while they were on their way, the groom arrived. And with him entered the bridesmaids who were ready for the wedding dinner. And then the door was closed.
After that, the other bridesmaids came too. They said, "Sir! Sir! Open the door for us!"
But he answered him: "What I am going to tell you is true. I do not know you."
Therefore, stay alert. You do not know the day or the hour when the bridegroom will come. A story of three servants.
Again, this is what the kingdom of heaven will be like. There was a man traveling who called his servants and entrusted his possessions to them.
He gave $10,000 to one person. He gave $4,000 to another person. And he gave $2,000 to the third person. The man gave each servant the amount that he knew that they could manage. And then after that he went on his journey.
The server who took the ten thousand dollars immediately went and invested his money. He earned an additional ten thousand dollars.
The person who won $4000 won an additional $4000.
The man who took two thousand dollars walked away, dug a hole in the ground, and hid his master's money in it.
After a long time, the master of these slaves returned. He liked to collect all the money they had earned.
The man who took the $10,000 brought another $10,000 with him. He said, "Sir, you trusted me and gave me $10,000. Look, I made another $10,000."
His master answered, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful over a few things, and I will make you ruler over many things. Come and share your master's joy!"
The man to whom $4000 had been given also came. He said, "Master, you trusted me and gave me $4000. Look, I have earned another $4000."
His master answered, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful over a few things, and I will make you ruler over many things. Come and share your master's joy!"
And then, the man who had received $ 2000 came. He said: "Sir, I knew that you are a hard man. You reap where you did not sow. You gather the crops where you did not plant the seeds."
That's why I was afraid. I went out and hid your money, $2000, in the ground. Look, this is your money.
His masters answered, "You wicked and lazy servant! Do you know that I harvest where I did not sow, and gather where I did not scatter seed?"
So, you should have put my money in the bank. When I returned, I would have received it with interest.
"And then his lord said to the other servants who worked with him: 'Take the two thousand dollars he has and give them to the one who has twenty thousand dollars.'"
Everyone has more to give. To have more than what is needed. And what happens to those who have nothing? Even those who have, can have it taken away.
Cast out this worthless servant into the outer darkness, where men shall weep and gnash their teeth.' The sheep and the goats.
The Son of Man is coming in his glory. All the angels will come with him. And then he will sit on his throne in heaven's glory.
All nations will be gathered before him. And he will separate the people into two groups. It will be like a shepherd who separates the sheep from the goats.
The sheep are grazing to his right and the goats to his left.
And then the King speaks to those on his right and says: 'My father, your blessing. Come and take what is yours. This is the kingdom prepared for you from the creation of the world.'
I was hungry. And you gave me something to eat. I was thirsty. And you gave me something to drink. I was a stranger. And you invited me in.
I needed clothes. And you gave them to me. I was sick. And you took care of me. I was in prison. And you came to visit me.
And after that, the people who did good deeds will answer him: O our Lord, when did we see you hungry and we gave you food? When did we see you thirsty and we gave you drink?
When did we see you as a stranger and invite you in? When did we see you in need of clothes and give them to you?
"When have you ever seen us visit you when you were sick or in jail?"
The King will answer: "What I tell you is the truth. Whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers of mine, you did it for me."
"Then He will say to those on His left, 'Depart from Me, all you who are under the curse, and go away into the eternal fire which has been prepared for the devil and his angels.'
I was hungry. But you did not give me anything to eat. I was thirsty. But you did not give me anything to drink.
I was a stranger, but you did not invite me in. I needed clothes, but you did not give me anything. I was sick and in prison, but you did not care for me.
And they will also answer: 'Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty or a stranger or in need of clothes or sick or in prison, and we did not help you?'"
He answers them, "What I tell you is true. Whatever you did not do for one of the least of these, you did not do for me."
And after that, they will go to be punished forever. But those who did good, will receive eternal life.
The plan was to kill Jesus.
And when Jesus had finished all this talk, he said to his disciples:
As you don't know, Easter will be in two days. The Son of Man will be handed over to be crucified.
And afterward, the high priests met with the elders of the people. And they met in the palace of Caiaphas, the high priest.
They planned to seize Jesus by guile. They intended to kill him.
"But it is not during the feast," they said. "The people might cause an uproar." A woman pours perfume on Jesus.
Jesus was in Bethany. He was in the house of a man named Simon, who had a skin disease.
A woman came to Jesus with an alabaster jar of very costly perfume. She poured it on his head as he sat at the table.
And when the students saw this, they were angry. And they said to them: "Why is there so much waste?"
The perfume could have been sold for a high price. The money could have been given to the poor.
Jesus was aware of this. That is why he said to them, "Why do you bother this woman? For she has done a beautiful thing for me.
There will always be poor people with you. But I will not always be with you.
She sprayed perfume on my body to prepare me for burial.
What I am about to tell you is true. What she has done will be remembered everywhere that this good news is spread throughout the world. She will be remembered for her sake." Judas agrees to betray Jesus
One of the twelve disciples went to the high priests. His name was Judas Iscariot.
He said, "What will you give me if I hand Jesus over to you?" And they paid him thirty pieces of silver.
And since then, Judas had been watching for the opportune time to hand Jesus over to them. The Lord's Supper
This was the first day of the Feast of Unleavened Bread. And the disciples came to Jesus, saying to Him, "Where do You want us to prepare for You to eat the Passover?"
He answered them, "Go into the city to a certain man, and say to him, 'The Teacher says: My time is near; I will keep the Passover at your house with my disciples.'"
And so the disciples did as Jesus had told them. And they prepared the Passover meal.
And when evening came, Jesus was reclining at the table with the twelve disciples.
And as they were eating, he said, "The one who is about to betray me is the one who is truly with me."
Don't make the students very sad. And they started saying to him, one after another: "It wasn't me, sir, right?"
Jesus answered, "The one who dipped his hand with me in the bowl, he is the one who will betray me."
Just as it is written of him, the Son of Man must suffer. Woe to the one who betrays the Son of Man. It would be better for him if he had never been born.
It was Judas who was going to betray him. He said, "Surely not I, Rabbi, am I?" Jesus answered, "You have said so."
And as they were eating, Jesus took bread. And when He had given thanks, He broke it and gave it to His disciples, and said, "Take, eat; this is My body."
And after that, he took the cup. And he thanked and gave it to them. And he said, "All of you drink from it."
This is my blood, which represents the new covenant. It was poured out to forgive the sins of many people.
Here's what I can tell you. From this moment on, I will no longer drink wine with you until the day we drink together in the kingdom of Puya."
And afterward, they sang a hymn and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus said that Peter would fail.
Jesus said to them, "This very night before the rooster crows, you will deny me three times because of me. For it is written, 'And the Lord said, "Surely My covenant with day and night will not be broken, as long as the earth remains. But you, be not dismayed; for the Lord is with you, and he will not fail you or forsake you.'" (Zechariah 13: 7).
But after my resurrection from the dead, I will precede you to Galilee."
Peter replied, "All the others might abandon you, but I won't, we never do such things."
"What I am about to tell you is true," Jesus answered. "It will all happen tonight. Before the rooster crows, you will deny me three times that you know me."
Peter said, "I may have to die with you, but I will never say that I don't know you." All the other disciples said the same thing. Jesus' Prayer in Gethsemane
And then Jesus walked with his disciples to a place called Gethsemani. He said to them: "Sit here while I go over there and pray."
And he took with him Peter and the two sons of Zebedee. And he began to feel sorrow and grief.
And He told them afterward, "My soul is very sorrowful, to the point of death. Stay here and keep watch with Me."
He walked a little further. And then he fell face forward to the ground. He prayed saying, "My Father, if it is possible, let this cup of suffering be taken away from me. But let your will be done, not mine."
Then he came back to his disciples and found them sleeping. He said to Peter, "Could you not watch with me for one hour?"
Watch and pray. In this way you will not fall into temptation when you are offered something tempting. The spirit is willing. But the body is weak."
Jesus walked a little way. He prayed, saying, "My Father, is it possible for this cup to be taken away? But if I must drink it, let Your will be done."
And then he came back. And he found them asleep again. They couldn't keep their eyes open.
And so he left them and went away once more. And for the third time he prayed and asked for the same thing.
Then he returned to the disciples and said to them: "Are you still sleeping and taking your rest? Look, the hour is near, and the Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of sinners."
Arise! We must go! Here comes he that betrayeth me unto them!" The arrest of Jesus.
And while Jesus was speaking, Judas arrived, one of the twelve disciples. A large crowd accompanied him, carrying swords and clubs. The chief priests and elders of the people were those who sent them.
Judas, who was going to betray Jesus, had agreed with them on a sign. He said, "The one I give a kiss is the man; seize him."
And immediately Judas went to Jesus and said, "Greetings, Teacher," and he kissed him.
Jesus answered, "My friend, do as you have come to do." Then the men approached and seized Jesus and arrested him.
At that time, one of Jesus' disciples drew his sword. He struck the servant of the high priest, cutting off his ear.
Jesus said to him, "Put your sword back into its place, for all those who take up the sword will perish by the sword."
Do you think I can't ask my father to help me? He can send an army of more than 70,000 angels immediately.
How can the holy books be fulfilled? They say it must happen this way."
At that time, Jesus spoke to the crowds. He asked them, "Do I look like I'm leading a rebellion? Were you supposed to come armed with swords and clubs to arrest me? I used to sit and teach in the Temple every day, and you never arrested me."
But all this happened so that the prophecies of the prophets would be fulfilled." Then all the disciples left him and fled. Jesus is led to the Sanhedrin council
The people who arrested Jesus took him to Caiaphas, who was the high priest. And that day they were assembled together with him, the teachers of the law and the elders.
Peter was following him at a distance, and he reached the house of the high priest. He entered and sat down with the guards to see what would happen.
And the chief priests and all the council of the Sanhedrin were seeking evidence against Jesus to condemn him to death, for they desired to put him to death.
They didn't find any evidence, even though many false witnesses came. In the end, two more witnesses came.
They said, "This man said, 'I am able to destroy my Lord's temple and to rebuild it in three days.'"
And after that, the high priest stood up. And he asked Jesus: "Do you not intend to answer? What are these charges that these men have brought against you?"
Jesus remained silent. The high priest said to him, "I adjure you by the living God, tell us if you are the Christ, the Son of God."
"Yes, the things are as you have said," Jesus answered. "But I tell all of you: In the coming days, you will see the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of the Powerful One. You will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven."
And after that, the high priest tore his clothes and said, "This man has spoken blasphemous words against God! Why do we need any more witnesses? You have heard him blaspheme."
"What do you think?" "He must die!" they answered.
And after that, they spat in his face. And they hit him with their fists. And others slapped him.
They said, "Prophecy to us, O Christ, who is he that smote thee?" Peter says that he does not know Jesus.
Peter was sitting in the courtyard. A servant girl came to him and said, "You also were with Jesus the Galilean."
But in front of them all, Peter said he was not. "I do not know what you are talking about," he said.
And after that, he went up to the gate that leads into the house. Another woman saw him there. And she told the people, "This man was with Jesus of Nazareth."
He said again that it wasn't so. He swore, "I don't know this man!"
After a while, the people who had been standing there had moved to Peter and said to him, "Surely you are one of them, for how you speak gives you away."
And Peter began to curse and swear, saying, "I don't know this man!" And at that moment the cock crowed.
Peter remembered what Jesus had said to him. Jesus had said, "The cock will not crow until you have denied me three times." Peter went out and wept bitterly.
Judah hangs himself.
It was early in the morning. And all the chief priests and the elders of the people agreed to kill Jesus.
They bound him and led him away. Then they handed him over to Pilate, the governor.
Judah, who handed him over, saw how they condemned Jesus to death. He felt shame and profound sorrow for what he had done. And so he returned the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests and the elders.
"I have sinned," he said. "I have handed over an innocent man." "What is that to us?" they answered. "That is your affair."
And so Judas threw the money in the temple and left. And after that he went and hanged himself.
The high priests collected the money. They said, "It is not right to put this money in the temple treasury. This is blood money. This money was paid for a man to be killed."
That's how they decided to use the money to buy pottery land. People from other countries will be buried there.
And for this reason, this place is called Blood Field until this day.
And so the words spoken by the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. He had said, "Take thirty pieces of silver, that is the price which the sons of Israel have set upon him."
And they took the minas and went to trade with them. And when the king came to inspect the accounts, he called the first servant
Jesus was standing before the governor. The governor asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "Yes, it is as you say."
And when the high priests and elders accused him, he did not answer.
And then Pilate asked him, "Don't you hear the charges they brought against you?"
Jesus, however, answered none of the charges, not even a single accusation. The governor was very astonished.
It was the custom of the governor at Passover to release a single prisoner. The people could choose the one they wanted.
At that time they had a notorious prisoner called Barabbas.
And as the crowd gathered, Pilate said to them, "Whom do you want me to release for you? Barabbas? Or Jesus who is called the Christ?"
Pilate knew that the leaders were jealous. He knew that this was the reason they had handed Jesus over to him.
And while Pilate was sitting on the judgment seat, his wife sent him a message saying, "Have nothing to do with this righteous man; for in a dream this very night I have suffered greatly because of him."
The chief priests and elders stirred up the crowd to have Barabbas released and Jesus executed.
"Which of the two do you want me to release?" asked the governor. "Barabbas," they answered him.
"What shall I do then with the king of the Jews, whom you call Jesus?" Pilate asked. They all answered him, "Crucify him!"
"Why? What evil has he done?" Pilate asked. But they shouted all the louder, "Crucify him!"
Seeing that Pilate could see there was no point, and that the crowd was starting to get angry. That's why he brought a bowl of water and washed his hands in front of them and said: "I am innocent of this man's death. It is your responsibility!"
All the people responded: "We and our children will bear the responsibility for his death."
Pilate released Barabbas, while Jesus was flogged, then delivered to be crucified. The soldiers mocked Jesus.
The ruler's soldiers bound Jesus and brought him into the governor's palace, which they called the Praetorium. And the rest of the soldiers gathered around him.
They took his clothes and dressed him in a purple robe.
And after that, they gathered thorns and made a crown with it. They put it on his head. And they put a staff in his right hand. And after that, they knelt down before him and began to mock him. They said to him: "We honor you, King of the Jews!"
They spat on him. They hit him on the head with a stick again and again.
After mocking him, they stripped him of his robe and returned his clothing to him. And then they took him to crucify him. Jesus is crucified on the cross.
And while they were going out of the city, they found a man from Cyrene named Simon. They forced him to carry the cross.
They arrived at a place called Golgotha. The word Golgotha means the place of the skull.
There they mixed the wine with myrrh and gave it to Jesus to drink. And when He tasted it, He would not drink it.
And when they crucified him, they divided his garments among them, casting lots.
They sat down and kept watching him there.
And they put above his head the accusation written against him. It was written: "This is Jesus, King of the Jews."
Two thieves were crucified with him, one on his right and one on his left.
Those who passed by there mocked Jesus, shaking their heads.
And they said, "What is this talk? Are you going to destroy the temple and rebuild it in three days? Save yourself! Come down from the cross, if you are the Son of God!"
And so it was understood, the high priests and teachers of the law and the elders began to mock him.
"He saved other people," they said. "But he can't save himself! He is the King of Israel! He will come down from the cross right now, and we will believe in him."
He trusts in God. May God deliver him now if He wants him. He is the one who said: "I am the Son of God."
In the same way, the two thieves who were crucified with Jesus ridiculed him. The death of Jesus
From noon until three o'clock, darkness covered all the earth.
About the third hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, "Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani?" which means, "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" (Psalm 22:1)
And some of those who were standing there heard Jesus cry out. They said, "He is calling for Elijah."
And immediately one of them ran and brought a sponge. And he filled it with sour wine and put it on a reed and offered it to Jesus to drink.
And the others said, "Leave him alone, let us see if Elijah will come to save him."
After Jesus cried out again in a loud voice, he died.
And behold, the veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom. And the earth shook, and the rocks were split.
Graves opened. And the bodies of many deceased saints came back to life.
They came out of the tombs. And after Jesus came back to life, they went to the holy city. And they appeared there to many people.
The Roman leader and the soldiers guarding Jesus saw the earthquake and all that happened. They were filled with fear and said, "This must surely be the Son of God!"
Not far away, many women were watching. They had followed Jesus from Galilee to care for his needs.
Mary Magdalene was among them. There was also Mary, the mother of James and Joseph. There was also the mother of the sons of Zebedee. The Burial of Jesus
And when the evening began to fall, a rich man from the city of Arimathia came. His name was Joseph. And he was a follower of Jesus.
He went to Pilate and asked him for Jesus's body. Pilate ordered that he be given it.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean piece of cloth.
They placed him in his new tomb, which he had carved out of the rock. A large stone was rolled in front of the entrance to the tomb. And then he left.
Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting there opposite the tomb. The guards at the tomb
The next day was the day after the preparation day. The chief priests and the Pharisees went to Pilate.
"Lord," they said, "we recall something that liar said when he was still alive. He claimed, 'After three days I will rise again.'"
If you do not do this, the students will come and steal the body. And after that they will tell the people that Jesus rose from the dead. This last lie is going to be worse than the first lie.
"Take with you some guards," Pilate answered. "Go. And increase the security of the tomb as much as you can."
And so they went and secured the tomb. They sealed the stone and placed guards to keep watch.
Jesus rises from the dead.
And so, Saturday passed. And dawn broke on the first day of the week. And Mary Magdalene went with the other Mary to see the tomb.
There was a great earthquake. And the angel of the Lord descended from heaven. And the angel went to the tomb. And he rolled away the stone and sat upon it.
His body was shining like lightning. His clothes were white as snow.
The guards were so afraid of him that they trembled and became like the dead.
The angel said to the women, "Do not be afraid, I know that you are looking for Jesus who was crucified."
He's not here! He got up, just as he said. Come see the spot where he was sleeping.
Go quickly! Tell His disciples: "He has been raised from the dead and has gone before you to Galilee. There you will see Him." This is what I told you.
And so, the women quickly ran out of the tomb. They were afraid, but they were also very happy. They ran to tell the disciples.
And suddenly, Jesus appeared to them and said to them, "Peace be upon you." They came to him and held his feet and prostrated to him.
And Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee, and there they will see me." The guards report to the chief priests.
And while the women were on their way, some guards entered the city. And they told the chief priests everything that had happened.
And when the high priests met with the elders, they made a plan. They gave a large sum of money to the soldiers.
They said to the soldiers: "We want you to say: 'His disciples came at night and stole his body while we were sleeping.'"
If the ruler hears about this report, we will pay him a bribe. That way you won't face any problems."
And so the soldiers took the money and did as they were told. And this story spread among all the Jews and is still in existence. The last commands that Jesus gave to his disciples
And after this, the 11 disciples went to Galilee. And they went to the mountain to which Jesus had told them to go.
As soon as they saw it, they worshipped it. But there were also those among them who had doubts.
And afterward, Jesus came to them and said, "All authority has been given to me in heaven and on earth."
So you must go and work so that all peoples may become your disciples. Baptize them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey all that I have commanded you. And be sure that I am with you always, to the end."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
This is the beginning of the gospel about Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
A long time ago, the prophet Isaiah wrote: "I will send my messenger to prepare the way before you." (Malachi 3:1)
Here is a messenger crying in the wilderness: 'Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight.' (Isaiah 40:3)
And that's how John came. He was baptizing people in the desert. And he was how to preach to people to be baptized and move away from their sins. And that's how God wants to forgive them.
All the people living in the countryside of Judea came to him, and even the people of Jerusalem came. And when they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the river Jordan.
John wore clothes made of camel hair. He wore a leather belt around his waist. He was eating locusts and wild honey.
This is what John was preaching about. "After me, someone will come who is more powerful than I am. I am not even worthy to stoop down and untie his sandals."
I baptize you with water; but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." The baptism and temptation of Jesus.
At that time, Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee. And John baptized him in the Jordan River.
As Jesus was coming up out of the water, he saw the heavens opened up and the Spirit descending on him like a dove.
He heard a voice speaking to him from the sky. It said to him, "You are my son, and I love you. I am very pleased with you."
And immediately, the Holy Spirit sent Jesus into the wilderness.
Jesus was in the desert for 40 days. There the devil tempted him. Wild animals did not harm Jesus. Angels took care of him. Jesus chooses the first disciples.
After John the Baptist was imprisoned, Jesus went into Galilee and began to preach the gospel of God.
"The time has come," he said. "The kingdom of my Lord is near. Repent of your sins and believe the good news!"
Jesus was walking one day by the Sea of Galilee. And there he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Jesus said, "Come, follow Me, and I will make you fishers of men."
And immediately they dropped their lines and followed him.
And then Jesus walked a little further. As he was walking, he saw James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in a boat preparing their nets.
And He called them immediately. And they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired hands, and they followed Jesus. Jesus Expels an Unclean Spirit
Jesus and those with him walked to Capernaum. And when the Sabbath came, he entered the synagogue. And he began to teach there.
People were astonished at his teaching. He taught them as one who has authority, not like their scribes.
At that time, a man cried out in their mosque. An evil spirit had possessed him. He said:
"What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Did you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the Holy One of God!"
"Be quiet!" Jesus said sternly. "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit shook the man violently. And after that, it came out of him with a scream.
Everyone was amazed. And they said to each other, "What is this? A new teaching! And with authority! Even the evil spirits submit to his commands and obey him."
The news about Jesus spread rapidly throughout Galilee. Jesus heals many people.
Jesus and those who were with him went out from the temple. And they went along, with James and John, toward the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying down on the bed. She was feverish. They told Jesus about her.
And he carried her. He took her hand and helped her stand up. And the fever left her. And after that, she started serving them.
In the evening, after sunset, the people brought to Jesus all the sick. And they also brought all the people who were possessed by demons.
All the people in the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They had all kinds of diseases. And he also drove out many demons. But he did not let the demons speak, because they knew who he was. Jesus prays in a quiet place.
It was very early in the morning, and the darkness was still over all. Jesus rose and left the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. And there he prayed.
Simon and his companions went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they shouted, "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go to some other place. I want to go to the neighboring towns. I must preach there also. For this reason I have come."
And so he went throughout all the region of Galilee, preaching in their synagogues and casting out demons. Jesus heals a man who had a skin disease.
A man who had a skin disease came to Jesus. Jesus told him to kneel down. He said, "If you are willing to make me clean, it is within your power."
Jesus was filled with profound anxiety. He reached out and touched the man. "I am willing to do this," he said. "Be 'clean'!"
Right away, the illness left him. He recovered and went out.
Jesus immediately expelled him. He gave the man a strong warning.
He said, "Don't tell anyone about this. Go and present yourself to the priest. Make the sacrifices that Moses commanded. This will be proof for the priest and for the people that you are 'pure'."
As for the man, he went out and immediately began to speak. And he spread the news to everyone. And for this reason, Jesus was no longer able to enter the city openly. And He remained outside in secluded places. But the people continued to come to Him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
After a few days, Jesus returned to Capernaum. And the people heard that he had come home.
There was a large crowd of people gathered together, so that there was no room for them. There was not even any room outside the door. And Jesus preached the word to them.
Four of those who came were carrying a man who could not walk.
What they couldn't get near to Jesus because of the crowd. That's why they dug a hole in the roof above Jesus and lowered the man down on a mat.
Jesus saw their faith, and said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
Some law professors were sitting there. They were thinking,
Why is this man talking like this? He's saying something very bad! Only God can forgive sins!
And Jesus immediately knew what they were thinking, and said to them, "Why do you reason about these things?
Is it easier to say to this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? Or to say, "Rise, take up your mat and walk"?
"I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
"I'm telling you," he said, "get up. Take your mat and go home."
The man stood up and took his mat. Then he walked away, while everyone was watching him. All the people were amazed. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this before!" Jesus Chooses Levi
And Jesus returned again to the side of the Sea of Galilee. And a great multitude came to Him. And He began to teach them.
And as he was walking, he saw Levi, the son of Alphaeus. Levi was sitting in the tax collector's booth. Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And Levi got up and followed him.
And after that, Jesus was dining at the house of Levi. And a great number of tax collectors and sinners were dining with him and his disciples. And they were part of the large crowds that were following Jesus.
There were some teachers of the law who were Pharisees. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. They asked his disciples: "Why does he eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard these words, and he said to them, "The healthy do not need a doctor, but the sick do. I did not come to call the righteous to repent, but sinners." Jesus is asked about fasting.
The disciples of John and of the Pharisees do not eat. Some people came to Jesus and said to him, "The disciples of John fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees also fast. But your disciples do not fast. Why do your disciples not fast?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? They cannot fast as long as he is with them.
The time it will take for the bridegroom to come from them. On this day, they will not eat anything.
People do not sew a new piece of cloth onto old clothes. If they do this, the new piece will tear away from the old piece. And thus the hole will become even larger.
People don't put new wine into old wineskins. If they do, the wine will burst the wineskins. And the wine will be lost, and the wineskins too. No, everyone puts new wine into new wineskins." Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
On a Saturday, Jesus was walking with his disciples in the wheat fields. And the disciples began to pluck the heads of wheat.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look! This man is doing what is unlawful on the Sabbath. Why then do your disciples do what is unlawful on the Sabbath?"
He answered, "Have you never read what David did? He and his men were hungry. They needed food."
This was the time when Abiathar was the high priest. David entered the house of God and ate the holy bread. Only the priests were allowed to eat it. And David gave some of it to his men also.
And Jesus said afterward, "The Sabbath was made for man, and not man for the Sabbath."
Therefore, the Son of Man is also the Lord of the Sabbath.
Healing on Saturday.
At another time, Jesus entered the synagogue. And there was a man whose hand was withered and deformed.
Some Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They were watching him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus spoke to the man who had a withered and crooked hand. He said to him, "Stand in front of everyone."
And after this, Jesus asked them, "What does the Law say should be done on the Sabbath? Is it right to do good, or right to do evil? Is it right to save life, or right to take life?" But no one answered him.
Jesus looked around at them angrily. He was very upset because their hearts were hardened. And then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and his hand was restored as it was before.
And after this the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus, seeking to kill him. The crowds follow Jesus.
Jesus walked with his disciples to the Sea of Galilee. A large crowd from Galilee followed them.
People heard about all the things Jesus was doing, and many people came to him. They came from Judea and Jerusalem and Idumea, and from the region east of the Jordan River, and from the area around Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus asked his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. That way, people wouldn't gather around him.
Jesus healed many people. That's why those who were sick were pushing themselves to touch him.
When the people possessed by evil spirits saw him, they fell down before him. The spirits cried out, "You are the Son of God!"
Jesus commanded them not to tell anyone about his identity. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up into the mountain. And He called the people to come to Him, and they came.
And he appointed 12 of them and called them apostles. And from then on, they were with him. And he would send them out too to preach.
They had the authority to cast out demons.
And so Jesus appointed the twelve disciples. Simon was one of them. And Jesus gave him the name Peter.
There was James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. Jesus gave them the name Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
And there were also Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, James the son of Alphaeus. And there was Thaddaeus and Simon the Zealot.
Judas Iscariot was one of them too. He was the one who later agreed to betray Jesus to his enemies. Jesus and Beelzebub
Jesus entered a house. The people gathered again. The crowd was so large that Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
His family heard about this. They went to take him with them. They said, "This one has no sense."
There were some teachers of the law who came down from Jerusalem. They said, "This man is controlled by Beelzebul, and by the prince of demons he is driving out demons."
And so Jesus called them, and began to speak to them in parables, saying: How can Satan cast out Satan?
If the kingdom is at war with itself, it cannot endure.
If a family is divided, it cannot stand.
